---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Technical Product Management · AI Engineering</p>
      <h1>I build systems people can understand, trust, and operate.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I work at the intersection of product, engineering, and trustworthy AI systems—especially the layer between a compelling demo and software that can survive real workflows, constraints, and failure modes.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button" href="/work/">See selected work</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/harness-engineering/">Explore Harness Engineering</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Selected work</p>
      <h2>Evidence, not a giant portfolio.</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        The site focuses on a small number of public projects that demonstrate different parts of how I work.
      </p>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Personal Memory Bank</h3>
          <p>Persistent project context and governance for AI coding assistants, with explicit authority, freshness, provenance, hooks, diagnostics, and CI guardrails.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev/personal-memory-bank">View on GitHub →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>AI Code Review Agent</h3>
          <p>Local, privacy-conscious multi-agent code review with isolated specialist perspectives, structured findings, evidence handling, and multiple developer interfaces.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev/ai-code-review-agent">View on GitHub →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>PitLogic</h3>
          <p>A client-side BBQ telemetry and analytics product that demonstrates end-to-end product delivery outside the AI meta-layer.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev/pitlogic">View on GitHub →</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">How I think</p>
      <h2>Operational usefulness beats AI theater.</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Start with the workflow</h3>
          <p>Technology choices should follow the user problem and operating environment rather than become the project.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Bound authority</h3>
          <p>Consequential automation should have explicit scope, approval points, and deterministic controls where deterministic checks are possible.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Make failure visible</h3>
          <p>Useful systems expose uncertainty, errors, degraded states, and recovery paths instead of quietly guessing.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Outside the terminal</p>
      <h2>Nolan's Workshop</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        I also build physical things. Woodworking gets its own identity and project space without pretending it is the same discipline as software engineering.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/woodworking/">Visit the woodworking section →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
